{"kind":"report","text":"Independent exact-head review of client and deployment hygiene e90cc7c0e8dc0fdb0362fec603de6c8435e72569: changes requested for one P1 security finding. All 14 artifact statements and their exact source/test/documentation changes were inspected; the changed paths match the manifest. The candidate is based on a6330262; plan003 has since landed independently. No source in the canonical checkout was modified.\n\nP1 — SEC-05 remains reachable through the credential marker (packages/cli/src/git.ts:57 and :93; packages/cli/src/main.ts:318-327, :694-708). The room is deliberately treated as an untrusted supplier of remote/token values, but its Claim.lane also reaches the config file. applyLocal stores the returned lane without validating it, laneOf later reads it, and configureWorkspace interpolates it into marker() on the first line. A newline ends that comment and introduces another config section even when checkGrant accepts both the remote and token. This invalidates the report's assertion that the generated file is always exactly one setting.\n\nReproduction on the exact candidate: the fake Room returns Claim.lane = \"act_3_0a1b2c3d\\n[core]\\nsshCommand = checker-sentinel\\n#\". The test maps the later lane lookup to the original held lane, representing a malicious Room consistently serving that chosen ID. Workspace preparation and its grant otherwise use the original implementation, a valid remote, and the ordinary token. Claim exits 0 and workspace exits 0; `git config --get core.sshCommand` returns `checker-sentinel`. Only an inert setting was read; no injected command was executed. Checker fixture: /tmp/artroom-checker-sec-hygiene-v1-diagnostics/packages/cli/test/checker-hygiene-marker.test.ts, named 'checker: a room-supplied lane cannot inject settings through the credential marker'. Its exact-head assertion fails. Adding only a test-control canonical-lane check at configureWorkspace makes the same test pass; removing that control restores the failure. Logs: /tmp/artroom-checker-sec-hygiene-v1-marker.log, -marker-control.log and -marker-restored.log.\n\nRequired repair: make every interpolated marker field safe, including the room-derived lane and lease. Validate canonical identifiers and the numeric lease before any pending installation/config/credential changes, and enforce the same invariant at configureWorkspace's own boundary; or use a marker encoding that cannot introduce a new physical config line and update its ownership reader consistently. Add direct-boundary and end-to-end controls that reject or safely encode a malicious Room's marker metadata, assert no injected setting/credential/remote and no pending installation is created, and retain valid credential ownership/release behavior. Preserve the existing remote/token checks and their early placement. This closes the same untrusted Room-to-git-config boundary as SEC-05; it needs no reduction in functional scope.\n\nArchitecture: the PUBLIC_URL startup validation and separate endpoint byte caps are sound. MCP authenticates before reading the body and replays a bounded body to its existing handler; HTTPS preserves strict JSON parsing and status mapping. The extra http.ts change is necessary to implement the requested HTTPS limit. The Workspace ownership record and release fence remain intact for ordinary grants. The unsafe marker metadata boundary above prevents approval.\n\nSecurity: PUBLIC_URL has no fallback or deployable placeholder and is checked in both Worker and Room constructors. Strict remote normal-form and token grammars prevent the tested remote/token config injections without echoing rejected values. Both routes count streamed bytes, reject declared oversize bodies before reading, stop past 1 MiB, and permit exactly 1 MiB. These repairs are credited. Git config is still injectable through the marker, so SEC-05 is not yet complete. The README separately identifies unvalidated CLI login MCP command rendering as an unchanged follow-up; this review does not treat that as a newly introduced defect or silently discharge it.\n\nSimplification: writing the private credential directly is a reasonable documented departure from the request to use git config arguments: the latter would put the bearer on a process command line. The real Git read-back control proves the admitted remote/token characters yield one setting. That evidence must extend to the whole file, including the ownership marker. A narrow metadata guard or safe marker encoding is sufficient; no new workflow or permission step is needed.\n\nIndependent validation: fresh npm ci, root typecheck and root npm test exit 0 on the clean exact candidate (2,032 passed, one skipped; Git Node 221, Room Node 138, Room workerd 400). All four targeted production test files first pass unchanged in a separate diagnostics clone (CLI 31, Room Node 13, Room workerd 6, MCP workerd 4). Independently broke all 21 reported guards, plus HTTP and MCP \u003e versus \u003e= boundary guards: all 23 produce named behavioral failures with no compilation/module failures. Exact production source was restored; all four targeted sets pass again. The separate marker diagnostic remains an exact-source runtime failure and its test-only control passes. No deploy, real provider request, or live credential was used.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:981253a8e07a838a2696a2f6f7dfa57d46181eae","binding":"assigned","head":"e90cc7c0e8dc0fdb0362fec603de6c8435e72569","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:82bdf8e1d9973ac251eeebab34f04de6822b1d10\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d3f360a90b4b5ef5589d9917bc6e99b2cf777d3f\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b42eef16fc3fd1b340902b56d30c560be8d3a8d4","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}